# 007 — Autonomous Browser QA & Visual Verification

## Purpose
Ensures that all user interface implementations and visual changes are thoroughly validated in real browser rendering environments before any task is marked as complete.

---

## 1. Mandatory Viewport Verification Matrix

When building or modifying UI pages, components, or layouts, the agent must verify visual rendering across two standard viewports:

| Viewport Target | Width $\times$ Height | Mandatory Checks |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Mobile Standard** | $375\text{px} \times 812\text{px}$ (iPhone SE / Standard Mobile) | - Zero horizontal scrollbar (`overflow-x: hidden`).<br>- Touch targets $\ge 44 \times 44\text{px}$.<br>- Hamburger / Mobile drawer opens smoothly.<br>- Text fits within screen bounds without truncation. |
| **Desktop Standard** | $1440\text{px} \times 900\text{px}$ (Standard Desktop) | - Balanced whitespace and maximum content container width.<br>- Multi-column grids render with proper gap spacing.<br>- Hover states activate correctly. |

---

## 2. Browser Verification Protocol

Before declaring any frontend task done, execute this automated or browser inspection sequence:

1. **Local Server Verification:** Ensure the development or preview server runs cleanly without compilation errors.
2. **Console Error Audit:** Open the target route and inspect browser console logs.
   - **Zero Red Errors:** There must be $0$ unhandled exceptions, hydration mismatches, missing React keys, or broken network 404s.
3. **Interactive Flow Check:**
   - Test form submissions (valid submission produces success toast/modal; invalid submission highlights errors).
   - Test modal dialogs, drawers, and dropdown toggles.
   - Test keyboard navigation (`Tab` cycle through interactive elements).
4. **Visual Artifact Documentation:** When possible or requested, capture screenshot evidence of the rendered interface at both mobile and desktop viewports.
